            |And the sense of being a jumped-up parvenu princess who could never replace Alissa makes her feel even worse
        ]
        |and despite her public[etheryn.highConfidence
            |
            | attempts at
        ] poise and despite all the help she has, the endless management and minor crises all take their toll. Heavy is the head, after all, that wears the crown[etheryn.crowned
            |
            | — even if she doesn't technically do that yet
        ]
    ]. What's more[etheryn.highConfidence
        |
        |, to your mild surprise
    ], she worries just as much about you and your safety while you're out adventuring[etheryn.pregStage 1
        |, especially given the precious bab[var0 2
            |ies
            |y
        ] that are along for the ride with you
    ]. [party.has etheryn
        |Even though she's at your side, it doesn't always make her feel any better; sometimes, it makes it worse. She sees you face danger time and again and [etheryn.lowConfidence
            |is terrified
            |worries endlessly
        ] that someday you'll be in peril where she <i>won't</i> be able to help you.
        |Every day without you feels like an eternity; nights with you, like this one, are over all too soon. And the thought that you're facing danger without her to help save you from it tears her apart.
    ][etheryn.numKids 1
        | Most of all, though, she worries for the health of your young [etheryn.numKids 2
            |children
            |child
        ], about whether you'll both be able to stay alive for them, and whether she has what it takes to be a good mother.
    ]
    |To say Ryn's terrified of her sister would be [etheryn.lnhConfidence
        |an understatement of dramatic proportions
        |a overstatement, but not by much
        |overstating the case a bit, but the worry yet remains
    ]. Despite the courage that it took to run away and rally help against Alissa, the elven princess is[etheryn.highConfidence
        | still
    ] deeply troubled by whether she has what it takes to save her people and fix everything. But it's not just the big worries. Ryn's also far from home for the first time in her life, and as bad as that home was for her[etheryn.lowConfidence
        |, the shy, beaten-down elf is almost as terrified of the great unknown
        | it was all she ever knew before she met you
    ].
]

There's no interrupting her as the words come tumbling out, but you don't want to do that anyway. Instead, you let her unburden herself, worry by worry, drain out everything that clouds her mind and speak its name so that she feels it less intensely. Only then, when she finally stops, do you offer her reassurance. 

Sometimes, when a worry calls for a logical response, you give one. But for the most part[flags.WC_CLEAR
